ell (h,c_h). It is the lowest cell of column c_h belonging to U. Any rectangle covering (h,c_h) cannot extend below row h, because the uncovered square (r_{c_h},c_h) = (h+1,c_h) must remain uncovered. Hence the rectangle’s bottom side lies on the horizontal grid line between rows h and h+1. Distinct heights give distinct bottom sides, and a rectangle possesses only one bottom side. Therefore each height h∈{1,…,n−1} forces a distinct rectangle. Thus any tiling of U uses at least n−1 rectangles.\"\n\nInterpretation: For each height h, there is a unique column c_h where the upper region has height h (i.e., r_{c_h} = h+1). The lowest cell in that column's upper region is (h,c_h). Any rectangle covering that cell must have its bottom side at row h (i.e., the line between rows h and h+1). Since each rectangle has only one bottom side, it can be the rectangle for at most one height. So we need at least n-1 rectangles. This is a standard argument used in many tiling lower bound proofs (like covering a Young diagram). It is valid.\n\nPotential subtlety: Could a rectangle covering (h,c_h) also cover (h',c_{h'}) where h' > h, but then that rectangle would have bottom side at row h, not at row h', so it would not be counted as the rectangle for height h'. However, the rectangle for height h' could be the same rectangle, but then its bottom side would be at row h, not at row h', which contradicts the requirement that the rectangle covering the lowest cell of column c_{h'} must have bottom side at row h' (since it cannot extend below row h'). Actually the rectangle covering (h',c_{h'}) cannot extend below row h', but it can extend below row h'? Wait, the rectangle covering (h',c_{h'}) cannot extend below row h' because below row h' is row h'+1 which is the uncovered square in column c_{h'}. So the rectangle's bottom side must be at row h' (the line between rows h' and h'+1). If the rectangle's bottom side is at row h < h', then it extends below row h', which would include the unc