, cover all squares except the diagonal, and satisfy the non‑overlap condition.  \n\n5. **Conclusion:**  \n   - The lower bound and the construction coincide, so the minimum number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   - For \\(n=2025\\), the minimum is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nConsider the \\(n\\times n\\) board whose unit squares are indexed by pairs \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\) (row \\(i\\), column \\(j\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBy the statement, each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares must be covered by rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines, and no two tiles may overlap.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad  \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery square not in \\(U\\) belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl([n]\\times[n]\\bigr)\\setminus U = L\\cup R,\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile may lie entirely inside \\(L\\) or entirely inside \\(R\\); it cannot intersect \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\nWe prove that any covering of \\(L\\) by axis‑aligned rectangles needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles; the same holds for \\(R\\). Consequently any tiling of the whole board needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  Reduction of \\(L\\) to a Ferrers board  \n\nLet \\(\\sigma\\) be a permutation of the rows such that  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(\\sigma(1))<\\pi(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <\\pi(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\n\nApply the row permutation \\(\\sigma\\) to the board: a rectangle \\([a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) (rows \\(a\\) through \\(b\\), columns \\(c\\) through \\(d\\)) is mapped to the rectangle \\([\\sigma^{-1}(a),\\sigma^{-1}(b)]\\times[c,d]\\).  \nThus the minimum number of rectangles needed to cover \\(L\\) equals the minimum number needed to cover its image  \n\n\\[\nL'=\\{(\\sigma^{-1}(i),j)\\mid (i,j)\\in L\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause of the ordering of the rows, we have \\(\\pi(\\sigma(k))=k\\) for each \\(k\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nL'=\\{(k,j)\\mid 1\\le j<k\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the strict lower‑triangular part of the board. This is a Ferrers diagram whose \\(k\\)-th row (counting from the top) contains exactly \\(k-1\\) squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Inductive lower bound for the lower‑triangular board  \n\n**Claim.** The lower‑triangular board \\(\\{(k,j):1\\le j<k\\le n\\}\\) cannot be partitioned into fewer than \\(n-1\\) axis‑aligned rectangles.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*  \n\n- **Base \\(n=1\\).** The board is empty, requiring \\(0=n-1\\) rectangles.  \n\n- **Inductive step.** Assume the claim holds for size \\(n-1\\).  \n  In the \\(n\\)-board the square \\((2,1)\\) lies in the topmost non‑empty row (row 2) and in column 1. Any rectangle covering \\((2,1)\\) may be extended downwards to include all squares \\((k,1)\\) with \\(k\\ge2\\); doing so yields a vertical rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  V=\\{(k,1)\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  of size \\((n-1)\\times1\\). Removing \\(V\\) from the board deletes column 1 from every row \\(k\\ge2\\). The remaining shape is exactly the lower‑triangular board of size \\(n-1\\) (rows \\(2,\\dots ,n\\) with column 1 removed). By the induction hypothesis, covering this remainder needs at least \\((n-1)-1=n-2\\) rectangles. Adding the rectangle \\(V\\) gives a total of at least  \n\n  \\[\n  1+(n-2)=n-1\n  \\]\n\n  rectangles. ∎\n\nThus any covering of \\(L\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  The right region  \n\nApply the same argument to the right region \\(R\\). After ordering the rows by decreasing \\(\\pi(i)\\) we obtain the strict upper‑triangular board \\(\\{(k,j):1\\le k<j\\le n\\}\\), which is symmetric to the lower‑triangular case. Hence \\(R\\) also requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.4  Global lower bound  \n\nSince the rectangles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\) are disjoint, the total number of tiles in any tiling of the whole board is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\). Then the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).\n\n- **Lower‑triangular part** \\(L=\\{(i,j):j<i\\}\\) is covered by the rectangles  \n\n  \\[\n  A_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>k,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\},\\qquad k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1.\n  \\]\n\n  Each \\(A_k\\) is a rectangle of dimensions \\((n-k)\\times k\\).\n\n- **Upper‑triangular part** \\(R=\\{(i,j):j>i\\}\\) is covered by the rectangles  \n\n  \\[\n  B_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\le k,\\;j>k\\},\\qquad k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1,\n  \\]\n\n  each of dimensions \\(k\\times (n-k)\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{A_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{B_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, together contain all squares except the diagonal, and each rectangle lies entirely inside the board with its sides on grid lines. Hence this yields a tiling with exactly  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nThus the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
