                "content": "**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(p:[n]\\to[n]\\) (with \\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) lies in column \\(p(i)\\).\n\n2. **Column‐wise analysis.**  \n   In any column \\(j\\) the only uncovered square is at the unique row \\(i_j\\) with \\(p(i_j)=j\\). Hence the covered cells of column \\(j\\) are precisely the \\(n-1\\) cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq i_j\\).\n\n3. **Lower bound.**  \n   A rectangular tile must consist of a contiguous set of rows. Therefore a tile that contains any cell of column \\(j\\) cannot contain the row \\(i_j\\); otherwise it would also contain the uncovered cell \\((i_j,j)\\). Consequently a tile covering column \\(j\\) can lie **only** either entirely above row \\(i_j\\) or entirely below it.  \n   - If \\(i_j\\) is an interior row (\\(2\\le i_j\\le n-1\\)), the column is split into two non‑empty contiguous blocks, so at least **two** tiles are required for that column.  \n   - If \\(i_j=1\\) or \\(i_j=n\\) (the uncovered cell is at the top or bottom of the column), a single tile suffices.  \n\n   Exactly one column has \\(i_j=1\\) (the column containing the uncovered square of row 1) and exactly one column has \\(i_j=n\\) (the column containing the uncovered square of row \\(n\\)). All other \\(n-2\\) columns need at least two tiles. Hence any tiling uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot (n-2)+2 = 2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   For each column \\(j\\) let \\(i_j\\) be the row of its uncovered square.  \n   - If \\(i_j>1\\), place a vertical \\(1\\times(i_j-1)\\) tile covering rows \\(1,2,\\dots,i_j-1\\) of column \\(j\\).  \n   - If \\(i_j<n\\), place a vertical \\(1\\times(n-i_j)\\) tile covering rows \\(i_j+1,i_j+2,\\dots,n\\) of column \\(j\\).  \n\n   These tiles are axis‑aligned, have sides on grid lines, never overlap, and together cover every covered cell. The total number of tiles is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\bigl( \\mathbf{1}_{i_j>1}+\\mathbf{1}_{i_j<n}\\bigr)\n   =2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe grid consists of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  \nA **tile** is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; a tile therefore corresponds to a product of a non‑empty interval of rows \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a non‑empty interval of columns \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThe condition “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square’’ forces the set of uncovered squares to be a **permutation matrix**:\n\n\\[\n\\exists\\;p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ bijective such that the uncovered square in row }i\\text{ is }(i,p(i)).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the set of covered squares is  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq p(i)\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A columnwise lower bound  \n\nFix a column \\(j\\). By (1) there is a unique row  \n\n\\[\ni_j\\;:=\\;p^{-1}(j)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwith the uncovered square \\((i_j,j)\\).  \nHence the covered squares of column \\(j\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\neq i_j\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsider any tile \\(T=I\\times J\\) that contains a cell of column \\(j\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) must avoid uncovered squares, the row interval \\(I\\) cannot contain the row \\(i_j\\); otherwise \\((i_j,j)\\in T\\), contradicting the definition of a tile.  \nTherefore \\(I\\) is a contiguous block of rows **entirely** above \\(i_j\\) (i.e. \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,i_j-1\\}\\)) or **entirely** below \\(i_j\\) (i.e. \\(I\\subseteq\\{i_j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).  \n\nConsequences for a single column:\n\n* If \\(i_j=1\\) (the uncovered square is at the top of the column), the set (4) is the contiguous block of rows \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); a single tile suffices.\n* If \\(i_j=n\\) (the uncovered square is at the bottom), the set (4) is \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); again a single tile suffices.\n* If \\(2\\le i_j\\le n-1\\), the set (4) splits into two non‑empty contiguous blocks,\n  \\(\\{1,\\dots ,i_j-1\\}\\) and \\(\\{i_j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n  No single tile can cover both blocks (it would have to contain row \\(i_j\\)), so **at least two** tiles are required for column \\(j\\).\n\nExactly one column contains the uncovered square of row 1 (the column \\(j=p(1)\\)), and exactly one column contains the uncovered square of row \\(n\\) (the column \\(j=p(n)\\)).  \nAll remaining \\(n-2\\) columns have interior missing rows.  \nHence any tiling of the covered squares uses at least  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{1+1}_{\\text{columns of rows }1\\text{ and }n}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{2\\cdot (n-2)}_{\\text{the other columns}}\n\\;=\\;2n-2\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let \\(i_j\\) be defined by (3).  \nPlace the following tiles:\n\n* If \\(i_j>1\\), place the vertical tile  \n\n  \\[\n  T_j^{\\text{up}} \\;=\\; \\{1,2,\\dots ,i_j-1\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n  which covers all cells of column \\(j\\) **above** the uncovered square.\n\n* If \\(i_j<n\\), place the vertical tile  \n\n  \\[\n  T_j^{\\text{down}} \\;=\\; \\{i_j+1,i_j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  which covers all cells of column \\(j\\) **below** the uncovered square.\n\nEach tile is a rectangle of width 1 and height either \\(i_j-1\\) or \\(n-i_j\\); its sides lie on grid lines.  \nBecause the column index \\(j\\) is different for distinct tiles, the tiles are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery covered cell \\((i,j)\\) belongs to exactly one of the two tiles (6)–(7) for its column, since by construction the only row omitted from column \\(j\\) is \\(i_j\\).  \nThus the tiles cover precisely the set \\(C\\) of (2).\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\mathbf{1}_{i_j>1}+\\mathbf{1}_{i_j<n}\\bigr)\n=\n\\underbrace{1+1}_{\\text{columns where }i_j=1\\text{ or }n}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{2\\cdot (n-2)}_{\\text{all other columns}}\n=2n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (5) shows that **any** tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nConstruction (6)–(7) achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Application to the given grid  \n\nFor the problem we have \\(n=2025\\).  \nHence the minimal number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
