s those with p(i) ≤ k+1, so i_ℓ ≥ i_{k+1}). So we have i_{k+1} ≤ i_ℓ ≤ b < i_{k+1}, contradiction. So indeed no rectangle can contain two distinct A_k. So each A_k must be in a distinct tile. So we need at least n-1 tiles for the A_k cells.\n\nSimilarly for B_k = (i_{k+1}, k). These are below the uncovered square of column k (since the uncovered square is at (i_k, k), and i_{k+1} > i_k). So B_k is covered. By symmetry, each B_k must be in a distinct tile. So we need at least n-1 tiles for the B_k cells. Since the sets A_k and B_k are disjoint (they are at different coordinates: A_k is at column k+1, B_k at column k), we need at least 2n-2 tiles.\n\nThus lower bound is correct.\n\nNow the construction: For each column j, we place vertical tile covering rows 1..i_j-1 (if i_j > 1) and vertical tile covering rows i_j+1..n (if i_j < n). These are disjoint vertical tiles covering all cells except the uncovered squares. Since each column has at most two tiles, total number of tiles is sum of (i_j > 1) + (i_j < n). Exactly one column has i_j = 1 (the column where uncovered square is at top row), exactly one column has i_j = n (the column where uncovered square is at bottom row). So total number of tiles is (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2.\n\nThus the construction uses exactly 2n-2 tiles, achieving the lower bound.\n\nThus answer 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to check Level 2: Reasoning validity. The solution uses valid mathematical methods: combinatorial reasoning, case analysis, monotonicity, contradiction, explicit construction. It also uses definitions of i_k, A_k, B_k, and a lemma. All methods are standard. No invalid reasoning like trial-and-error. So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. We need to check if there are any justification gaps or critical errors. The solution is fairly detailed. However, we need to check if any claims are not fully justified or if any steps are missing.\n\nPotential issues:\n\n- The solution defines i_k = max{i | p(i) ≤ k} for k = 1,