                "content": ".\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Model the uncovered squares.**  \n   The condition “exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column’’ forces the uncovered squares to be a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound (construction).**  \n   For every column \\(j\\) the cells above the uncovered square in that column form a vertical interval, and the cells below it form another vertical interval.  \n   Using these vertical intervals we obtain exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles (two for each column except the column whose uncovered square lies in the top row and the column whose uncovered square lies in the bottom row, which contribute only one rectangle).  \n   This gives a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n3. **Lower bound (necessity of \\(2n-2\\) tiles).**  \n   - Split the board into the **left part** \\(L=\\{(i,j):j<\\sigma(i)\\}\\) and the **right part** \\(R=\\{(i,j):j>\\sigma(i)\\}\\).  \n   - After sorting the rows by increasing \\(\\sigma(i)\\) the set \\(L\\) becomes a Young diagram whose row‑lengths are \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\). A Young diagram with distinct row‑lengths \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) cannot be partitioned into fewer than \\(n-1\\) axis‑parallel rectangles (each rectangle can reduce the maximal row‑length by at most one). Hence any tiling needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles to cover \\(L\\).  \n   - By symmetry the same argument shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(R\\).\n\n   - **Purification Lemma.**  \n     Let a rectangle \\(Q\\) cover cells from both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\). Write its column interval as \\([c,d]\\).  \n     For every row \\(i\\) covered by \\(Q\\) we have either \\(\\sigma(i)>d\\) (so \\(Q\\) lies left of the hole in that row) or \\(\\sigma(i)<c\\) (so \\(Q\\) lies right of the hole).  \n     Consequently the set of rows covered by \\(Q\\) splits into two (possibly empty) consecutive blocks:\n     \\[\n     I_{\\text{left}}=\\{i\\in I:\\sigma(i)>d\\},\\qquad\n     I_{\\text{right}}=\\{i\\in I:\\sigma(i)<c\\}.\n     \\]\n     Replacing \\(Q\\) by the two rectangles\n     \\[\n     Q_{\\text{left}}=I_{\\text{left}}\\times[c,d],\\qquad\n     Q_{\\text{right}}=I_{\\text{right}}\\times[c,d]\n     \\]\n     yields a valid tiling (both new rectangles avoid all holes).  \n     Starting from an optimal tiling, repeatedly apply this replacement to every mixed rectangle.  \n     Whenever a mixed rectangle is split, one of the two resulting pure rectangles can be merged with an existing pure rectangle having the same column interval (the merge does not create a hole because the two row‑intervals are disjoint).  \n     Hence there exists an optimal tiling that uses **only pure rectangles**, i.e. rectangles that lie entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).\n\n   - Since a pure tiling needs at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles, any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper and lower bounds coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the rows and columns of the board by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square,\nthere is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered\nsquares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery tile must be an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior contains no\npoint of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nFor a column \\(j\\) let \\(r_j=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\) be the row of the uncovered\nsquare in that column.\n\n*If \\(r_j>1\\)* the cells \\((1,j),\\dots ,(r_j-1,j)\\) lie **above** the hole.\nThey form a vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<r_j\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(r_j<n\\)* the cells \\((r_j+1,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\) lie **below** the hole.\nThey form a vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor the column whose hole is in the top row (\\(r_j=1\\)) only the “down’’\nrectangle is non‑empty; for the column whose hole is in the bottom row only the\n“up’’ rectangle is non‑empty.  All other columns contribute two non‑empty\nrectangles.  Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\\{V_j^{\\text{up}},V_j^{\\text{down}}\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, they are pairwise disjoint,\nand together they cover every unit square that is not in \\(U\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{there exists a tiling with }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Lower bound – the left and right parts  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) consists of the cells left of the uncovered square in each row,\n\\(R\\) the cells right of it; clearly \\(L\\cup R\\) is the set of all squares\nthat must be tiled.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 3.1 (left part needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles)\n\nSort the rows so that  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(r_1)<\\sigma(r_2)<\\dots <\\sigma(r_n).\n\\]\n\nThen the row‑lengths of \\(L\\) (the numbers of cells of \\(L\\) in each row) are  \n\n\\[\n0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(L\\) is a Young diagram whose rows have distinct lengths.\nConsider any partition of \\(L\\) into axis‑parallel rectangles.\nLet \\(h\\) be the maximal row‑length of \\(L\\); here \\(h=n-1\\).\nA rectangle can contain cells from at most one row of length \\(h\\),\nbecause any rectangle that contains two cells of that longest row would have\nto include the uncovered square of that row (the hole lies at the right end of\nthe row).  Hence each rectangle can reduce the maximal row‑length by at most\none, and at least \\(h=n-1\\) rectangles are necessary.\n\nThus any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3.2 (right part needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles)\n\nThe set \\(R\\) is the mirror image of \\(L\\) (replace each column \\(j\\) by\n\\(n+1-j\\)).  By the same argument as in Lemma 3.1, any partition of \\(R\\)\ninto rectangles requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Purification Lemma – mixed rectangles can be eliminated  \n\nA rectangle \\(Q\\) is called **pure left** if it is contained in \\(L\\),\npure right if it is contained in \\(R\\), and **mixed** otherwise.\n\nLet \\(Q\\) be a mixed rectangle.  Write its column interval as\n\\([c,d]\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\) and let \\(I\\) be the set of rows covered\nby \\(Q\\).  Because \\(Q\\) contains no uncovered square, for each\n\\(i\\in I\\) we have either \\(\\sigma(i)>d\\) (the whole column interval lies left\nof the hole in row \\(i\\)) or \\(\\sigma(i)<c\\) (the interval lies right of the\nhole).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nI=I_{\\text{left}}\\cup I_{\\text{right}},\\qquad\nI_{\\text{left}}=\\{i\\in I\\mid\\sigma(i)>d\\},\\;\nI_{\\text{right}}=\\{i\\in I\\mid\\sigma(i)<c\\},\n\\]\n\nand the two sets are disjoint and each is a (possibly empty) interval of\nconsecutive rows (the rows are consecutive because \\(Q\\) itself is a rectangle).\n\nDefine\n\n\\[\nQ_{\\text{left}}=I_{\\text{left}}\\times[c,d],\\qquad\nQ_{\\text{right}}=I_{\\text{right}}\\times[c,d].\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(Q_{\\text{left}}\\) and \\(Q_{\\text{right}}\\) avoid all uncovered squares,\nhence they are admissible tiles.  Moreover\n\n\\[\nQ=Q_{\\text{left}}\\cup Q_{\\text{right}},\\qquad\nQ_{\\text{left}}\\cap Q_{\\text{right}}=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus a mixed rectangle can be **split** into two pure rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   Existence of an optimal pure tiling  \n\nStart from a tiling that uses the minimum possible number of rectangles.\nAmong all such optimal tilings choose one that contains the maximum possible\nnumber of pure rectangles.  If a mixed rectangle were present, we could apply\nthe splitting operation (2) to obtain a tiling with the same total number of\nrectangles but with one more pure rectangle, contradicting the maximality.\nHence an optimal tiling can be assumed to consist only of pure left and pure\nright rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.   Completion of the lower bound  \n\nLet \\(t\\) be the number of rectangles in an optimal tiling.\nBy the previous paragraph we may assume the tiling is pure.\nThe pure left rectangles cover \\(L\\); by Lemma 3.1 at least \\(n-1\\) of them are\nnecessary.  The pure right rectangles cover \\(R\\); by Lemma 3.2 at least\n\\(n-1\\) of them are necessary.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nt\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.   Final answer  \n\nFrom (1) we have a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and from (3) no tiling can\nuse fewer.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
