ectangle lies entirely left) or c > sigma(i) (entirely right). So rows can be partitioned into left and right intervals. Because sigma is monotone after reordering, the sets I_left and I_right are consecutive intervals. So rectangle can be expressed as union of two rectangles: (I_left x J) and (I_right x J). So any mixed rectangle can be replaced by two pure rectangles. This may increase rectangle count, but they then merge pure rectangles that share same column interval and are vertically adjacent (i.e., same J, contiguous I). Merging reduces count. So after all possible merges, we have a tiling with only pure rectangles, with number of rectangles <= original number? Actually they claim \"there exists a tiling with no more rectangles than the original one that uses only pure rectangles.\" Let's check: Starting with original tiling, replace each mixed rectangle by two pure rectangles (increase count by at most number of mixed rectangles). Then merge pure rectangles that share same column interval and are vertically adjacent. Merging reduces count. So final count is at most original count + (#mixed) - (#merged). But #merged may be at least #mixed? Not necessarily, but they claim you can merge all possible vertical adjacencies, which may reduce count enough to be <= original. However, is it guaranteed? Let's think: Suppose you have a mixed rectangle that splits into two pure rectangles that are not vertically adjacent to any other pure rectangle with same J. Then you have increased count by 1 (since you replaced 1 rectangle with 2). If you cannot merge any, final count is original +1. So the claim that you can get a tiling with no more rectangles than original may not hold in all cases. But perhaps you can always merge each new pure rectangle with some existing pure rectangle of same J (maybe the original tiling had some pure rectangles covering those rows). Let's examine more carefully.\n\nThe purification procedure: Starting from any tiling, replace every mixed rect