n c and d (i.e., sigma(i) in (c,d)). Those rows cannot be in rectangle. So the rectangle's rows are two intervals separated by that gap. So the rectangle is actually two disjoint rectangles already? But they treat it as a single rectangle because it's defined as I x J where I is a set of rows that may be non-consecutive? Actually they defined rectangle Q = I x J where I = {a, a+1, ..., b} is a consecutive interval of rows. So rectangle Q must be contiguous in rows. So for Q to be mixed, the entire interval I must contain rows with holes both left and right of J. That means there must be at least one row i in I where sigma(i) < c, and at least one row i' in I where sigma(i') > d. Since sigma is a permutation, there must be rows in I where sigma(i) lies between c and d as well (by the intermediate value property? Not exactly, but if sigma is arbitrary, it's possible that sigma(i) jumps from left to right without covering intermediate columns). However, if sigma is monotonic after reordering rows, then sigma(i) is increasing with i. So if we reorder rows such that sigma(i) is increasing, then in that ordering, sigma(i) is monotonic. Then any interval I = [a,b] will have sigma(i) values ranging from sigma(a) to sigma(b). So if sigma(a) < c and sigma(b) > d, then sigma(i) for i in I will take all values between sigma(a) and sigma(b) (since sigma is a permutation, not necessarily consecutive but it will include some values between). Actually sigma is a permutation, so the values sigma(i) for i in [a,b] are some subset of {1,...,n} of size b-a+1. Since sigma is increasing, they are exactly sigma(a), sigma(a+1), ..., sigma(b). So they are consecutive integers. So if sigma(a) < c and sigma(b) > d, then there will be some i in [a,b] such that sigma(i) = c or sigma(i) = d or lies between c and d. So there will be a row where sigma(i) is in [c,d], which would be a hole inside rectangle Q, which is not allowed. So a mixed rectangle cannot exist after reordering rows to monotonic