ares are precisely  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nPermuting the rows by \\(\\pi\\) and the columns by \\(\\sigma\\) (i.e. relabelling row \\(i\\) as \\(\\pi(i)\\) and column \\(j\\) as \\(\\sigma(j)\\)) does not affect the possibility of a tiling, because the grid is symmetric.  \nHence we may assume without loss of generality that \\(\\sigma\\) is the identity; thus the uncovered squares are the diagonal squares\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Algebraic description of a tile  \n\nConsider a rectangular tile \\(T\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nR_T\\subseteq R,\\qquad C_T\\subseteq C\n\\]\n\nbe the sets of rows and columns occupied by \\(T\\).  \nSince the sides of a tile lie on grid lines, \\(T\\) consists exactly of the squares\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R_T,\\;j\\in C_T\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause no uncovered square may be covered, we must have\n\\[\nR_T\\cap C_T=\\varnothing .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nDefine the binary (0–1) column vectors  \n\n\\[\nu^{(T)}\\in\\{0,1\\}^n,\\qquad v^{(T)}\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\n\\]\n\nby  \n\n\\[\nu^{(T)}_i=\\begin{cases}1,&i\\in R_T,\\\\0,&i\\notin R_T,\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nv^{(T)}_j=\\begin{cases}1,&j\\in C_T,\\\\0,&j\\notin C_T.\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe indicator matrix of the squares covered by \\(T\\) is then the outer product  \n\n\\[\nM^{(T)}=u^{(T)}\\,(v^{(T)})^{\\!T},\n\\qquad\nM^{(T)}_{ij}=u^{(T)}_i\\,v^{(T)}_j=\n\\begin{cases}1,&i\\in R_T,\\;j\\in C_T,\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus each tile corresponds to a binary matrix of **rank 1** (over the reals).  \nCondition (1) guarantees that the diagonal entries of \\(M^{(T)}\\) are all zero.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Matrix equation for a tiling  \n\nLet the tiling consist of \\(k\\) tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_k\\).  \nSince every non‑diagonal square must be covered **exactly once**, we have\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=1}^{k} M