because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.\nThe square \\((r_k,k)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) and is the **rightmost** square of row\n\\(r_k\\) (its row contains exactly the columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\) in \\(L\\)).\n\nConsider any tile \\(T=I\\times J\\) that contains \\((r_k,k)\\).\nSince \\((r_k,k)\\) is the rightmost square of its row, the column interval \\(J\\) cannot\ncontain any column larger than \\(k\\); otherwise the hole \\((r_k,k+1)\\) would lie in\n\\(T\\).  Hence \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\nNow suppose a single tile contains two distinct squares\n\\((r_k,k)\\) and \\((r_{k'},k')\\) with \\(k<k'\\).\nBecause the column interval of the tile must contain both \\(k\\) and \\(k'\\),\nit must contain the column \\(k+1\\).  But the row \\(r_k\\) has its hole precisely at\ncolumn \\(k+1\\), so the tile would contain a hole – a contradiction.\nTherefore **no tile can cover two different squares of the set**\n\\[\nE:=\\{(r_k,k)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince the squares of \\(E\\) are all distinct and each must be covered, at least\n\\(|E|=n-1\\) tiles are required for \\(L\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3.  (Covering \\(R\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.)\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe region \\(R\\) is the mirror image of \\(L\\) under the transformation\n\\((i,j)\\mapsto (i,n+1-j)\\).  Applying Lemma 2 to this reflected board yields the\nsame lower bound for \\(R\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 4.  (Any covering of the whole board uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.)\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 1 the total number of tiles equals \\(t=t_L+t_R\\).  \nLemma 2 gives \\(t_L\\ge n-1\\) and Lemma 3 gives \\(t_R\\ge n-1\\); hence\n\\(t\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5.  (There exists a covering with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.)\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\si