as the left vertex class and the columns as the right vertex class of the complete bipartite graph \\(K_{n,n}\\), then (2) says that the edge set between \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) contains **no** edge of the perfect matching  \n\n\\[\nM=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a tile corresponds to a *biclique* (complete bipartite subgraph) of the graph  \n\n\\[\nG:=K_{n,n}\\setminus M .\n\\]\n\nConversely, any biclique of \\(G\\) determines a rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares.  \nHence a placement of tiles that covers every square of \\(G\\) **without overlap** is exactly a *partition* of the edge set of \\(G\\) into bicliques.\n\nConsequently the problem is equivalent to:\n\n> Determine the smallest number of bicliques needed to partition the edges of \\(G=K_{n,n}\\setminus M\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   A lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) bicliques\n\nWe prove the following.\n\n> **Lemma 1.** For every integer \\(n\\ge1\\), any biclique partition of \\(G=K_{n,n}\\setminus M\\) uses at least \\(2n-2\\) bicliques.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n*Base case \\(n=1\\).*  \n\\(G\\) has no edges, so zero bicliques are needed; \\(2\\cdot1-2=0\\). The statement holds.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the lemma true for size \\(n-1\\) (with \\(n\\ge2\\)).  \n\nLet \\(r\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(r)=1\\); i.e. the uncovered square in column\u202f1 lies in row \\(r\\).  \nConsider the subgraph obtained from \\(G\\) by deleting row \\(r\\) and column\u202f1.  \nThe remaining vertex sets are  \n\n\\[\nR'=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{r\\},\\qquad\nC'=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(i\\in R'\\) we have \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq1\\); thus \\(\\sigma(i)\\in C'\\).  \nThe restriction of \\(\\sigma\\) to \\(R'\\) is a bijection onto \\(C'\\); consequently the uncovered squares that survive the deletion form a perfect matching on \\(R'\\cup C'\\).  \nHence the induced subgraph is precisely  \n\n\\[\nG' = K_{n-1,n-1}\\setminus M',\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(M'\\) is that perfect matching.  \nBy the induction hypothesis any biclique partition of \\(G'\\) needs at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)-2 = 2n-4\n\\]\n\nbicliques.\n\nIt remains to account for the edges of \\(G\\) that are incident to the deleted row \\(r\\) or to the deleted column\u202f1.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_{r}= \\{(r,j)\\mid j\\neq1\\},\\qquad\nS_{1}= \\{(i,1)\\mid i\\neq r\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S_{r}\\) is a star centred at row \\(r\\); \\(S_{1}\\) is a star centred at column\u202f1.  \nObserve that **no** biclique of \\(G\\) can contain an edge of both \\(S_{r}\\) and \\(S_{1}\\):  \nif a biclique contained a vertex from each side, it would contain the pair \\((r,1)\\), which is precisely the missing edge of \\(M\\) and therefore forbidden.  \nThus the edges of \\(S_{r}\\cup S_{1}\\) must be covered by at least two distinct bicliques.\n\nEach of the two stars can be covered by a single biclique:\n\\[\n\\{r\\}\\times\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\quad\\text{covers }S_{r},\\qquad\n\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{r\\}\\bigr)\\times\\{1\\}\\quad\\text{covers }S_{1}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently any biclique partition of the whole graph \\(G\\) needs at least  \n\n\\[\n(2n-4)+2 = 2n-2\n\\]\n\nbicliques. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   An explicit partition with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\nWe now exhibit a tiling that attains the lower bound.\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile; it avoids the hole \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(j>1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j = \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) is a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile; it avoids the hole \\((j,j)\\).\n\nThe family \\(\\{H_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\mid j=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) consists of  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\npairwise disjoint rectangles.  \nEvery cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) lies either above the diagonal (\\(i<j\\)) and is covered by the unique horizontal tile \\(H_i\\), or below the diagonal (\\(i>j\\)) and is covered by the unique vertical tile \\(V_j\\).  \nThe diagonal cells are precisely the uncovered squares.  \nThus this construction yields a valid tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Minimality\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
