"**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Reduction to a permutation matrix.**  \n   - Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares forms a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \n2. **Decomposition into left and right regions.**  \n   - Define \\(L=\\{(i,j):j<\\pi(i)\\}\\) (squares left of the hole in their row) and \\(R=\\{(i,j):j>\\pi(i)\\}\\) (squares right of the hole).  \n   - Any axis\u2011aligned rectangle that avoids the holes lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\). Hence the tiling splits into a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).  \n3. **Lower bound (at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles).**  \n   - For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) there is a unique row \\(r\\) with \\(\\pi(r)=c+1\\); the square \\((r,c)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) and any rectangle covering it cannot extend to column \\(c+1\\). Consequently a tile of \\(L\\) has rightmost column exactly \\(c\\). Thus \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.  \n   - Symmetrically, for each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) there is a unique row \\(r\\) with \\(\\pi(r)=c-1\\); the square \\((r,c)\\) belongs to \\(R\\) and forces a tile of \\(R\\) whose leftmost column is exactly \\(c\\). Hence \\(R\\) also needs at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.  \n   - Therefore any tiling uses at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.  \n4. **Upper bound (construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles).**  \n   - Choose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\). The uncovered squares are the main diagonal.  \n   - For each column \\(c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\)) place a vertical rectangle covering all squares below the diagonal in that column (rows \\(c+1,\\dots ,n\\)).  \n   - For each column \\(c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n\\)) place a vertical rectangle covering all squares above the diagonal in that column (rows \\(1,\\dots ,c-1\\)).  \n   - This yields exactly \\(n-1\\) tiles for the lower part and \\(n-1\\) tiles for the upper part, totalling \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and it satisfies all requirements.  \n5. **Conclusion.** The lower and upper bounds coincide, so the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) the minimum is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nNumber the rows \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there is a function  \n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]  \nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) lies in column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\pi\\) is a bijection; i.e. \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n\nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nDefine  \n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]  \nClearly  \n\\[\nL\\cup R=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U,\n\\]  \nand \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Any tile lies wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\)\n\nLet \\(T=I\\times J\\) be an axis\u2011aligned rectangle (where \\(I,J\\) are intervals of rows and columns) that does not contain any uncovered square.  \nAssume for contradiction that \\(T\\) contains a square of \\(L\\) and a square of \\(R\\).  \nThen there exist \\(i,i'\\in I\\) and \\(j,j'\\in J\\) with \\(j<\\pi(i)\\) and \\(j'>\\pi(i')\\).  \nBecause \\(J\\) is an interval, it contains every integer between \\(j\\) and \\(j'\\); in particular it contains \\(\\pi(i)\\) or \\(\\pi(i')\\).  \nConsequently \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) or \\((i',\\pi(i'))\\) would belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(T\\) avoids all uncovered squares.  \nHence a tile cannot intersect both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\); it is contained entirely in one of them.  \n\nTherefore the whole tiling splits into a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\n##### Lemma 4.1 (columns of \\(L\\)).  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) there exists a tile of the tiling of \\(L\\) whose **rightmost** column equals \\(c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix such a column \\(c\\). Because \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, there is a unique row  \n\\[\nr_c\\quad\\text{with}\\quad \\pi(r_c)=c+1 .\n\\]  \nThe square \\((r_c,c)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) because \\(c<\\pi(r_c)=c+1\\).  \nAny rectangle covering \\((r_c,c)\\) cannot extend to column \\(c+1\\); otherwise it would contain the uncovered square \\((r_c,c+1)\\).  \nSince the column interval of a rectangle is contiguous, the rightmost column of that rectangle must be exactly \\(c\\). \u220e  \n\nBecause different columns have different rightmost columns, the tiles guaranteed by Lemma\u202f4.1 are distinct.  \nHence **at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover \\(L\\).**\n\n##### Lemma 4.2 (columns of \\(R\\)).  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) there exists a tile of the tiling of \\(R\\) whose **leftmost** column equals \\(c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix such a column \\(c\\). There is a unique row  \n\\[\ns_c\\quad\\text{with}\\quad \\pi(s_c)=c-1 .\n\\]  \nThe square \\((s_c,c)\\) lies in \\(R\\) because \\(c>\\pi(s_c)=c-1\\).  \nAny rectangle covering \\((s_c,c)\\) cannot extend to column \\(c-1\\); otherwise it would contain the uncovered square \\((s_c,c-1)\\).  \nThus the leftmost column of that rectangle is exactly \\(c\\). \u220e  \n\nAgain the tiles obtained for different columns are distinct, so **at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover \\(R\\).**\n\nCombining the two lemmas, any tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]  \n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Tiles covering the region below the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]  \n\nIt consists of all squares in column \\(c\\) that lie strictly below the diagonal; it contains no diagonal square.\n\n*Tiles covering the region above the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,c-1\\}.\n\\]  \n\nIt consists of all squares in column \\(c\\) that lie strictly above the diagonal; again it avoids the diagonal.\n\nThe rectangles \\(T_c^{\\text{down}}\\) (for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) are disjoint, as are the rectangles \\(T_c^{\\text{up}}\\) (for \\(c=2,\\dots ,n\\)).  \nTogether they cover every square of the board except the diagonal squares, and each of them is a legitimate axis\u2011aligned rectangle.  \n\nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles and the explicit construction achieving exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles show that the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
