n above the diagonal).  \n   - For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place a vertical rectangle covering the squares \\((i,c)\\) for \\(c+1\\le i\\le n\\) (the part of the column below the diagonal).  \n   - These \\(n-1\\) “above” rectangles and \\(n-1\\) “below” rectangles are pairwise disjoint, avoid all diagonal squares, and together cover every non‑diagonal square.  \n   - The total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).  \n\n5. **Conclusion.** The lower bound and the construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nNumber the rows \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a function  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) lies in column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\pi\\) is bijective; i.e. \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each column \\(c\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_c:=\\pi^{-1}(c)\\qquad(\\text{so }\\pi(r_c)=c).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Squares that force the rightmost / leftmost column of a tile  \n\nFor every \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:=(r_{c+1},c).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\pi(r_{c+1})=c+1>c\\), the square \\(L_c\\) lies **left** of the uncovered square in its row.\n\nFor every \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_c:=(r_{c-1},c).\n\\]\n\nHere \\(\\pi(r_{c-1})=c-1<c\\), so \\(R_c\\) lies **right** of the uncovered square in its row.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma: a tile covering \\(L_c\\) has rightmost column \\(c\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(T\\) be a rectangular tile that covers \\(L_c\\).  \nWrite the column interval of \\(T\\) as \\([a,b]\\) with \\(a\\le c\\le b\\).  \nIf \\(b\\ge c+1\\), then column \\(c+1\\) belongs to \\([a,b]\\).  \nBecause \\(T\\) also contains the row \\(r_{c+1}\\) (the row of \\(L_c\\)), the square \\((r_{c+1},c+1)\\) would be covered.  \nBut \\((r_{c+1},c+1)\\) is an uncovered square (it belongs to \\(U\\)), a contradiction.  \nHence \\(b\\le c\\). Since \\(c\\in[a,b]\\), we must have \\(b=c\\). ∎  \n\nConsequently the **rightmost column** of any tile covering \\(L_c\\) is forced to be exactly \\(c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Distinctness of the tiles covering the \\(L_c\\)\n\nAssume a single tile \\(T\\) covers two distinct squares \\(L_c\\) and \\(L_{c'}\\) with \\(c<c'\\).  \nBy Lemma 3 the rightmost column of \\(T\\) must be \\(c'\\).  \nThus the column interval of \\(T\\) contains the column \\(c+1\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) contains the row \\(r_{c+1}\\) (the row of \\(L_c\\)), the square \\((r_{c+1},c+1)\\in U\\) would be covered – impossible.  \nTherefore no tile can contain two different squares \\(L_c\\).  \nHence the \\(n-1\\) squares \\(\\{L_c\\mid1\\le c\\le n-1\\}\\) require **at least \\(n-1\\) distinct tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Symmetric statements for the squares \\(R_c\\)\n\nThe same reasoning, applied to the squares \\(R_c\\), yields:\n\n*Lemma 5.* Any tile covering \\(R_c\\) must have **leftmost column** exactly \\(c\\).\n\n*Corollary 6.* No tile can contain two distinct squares \\(R_c\\) (with different \\(c\\)).  \nThus the \\(n-1\\) squares \\(\\{R_c\\mid2\\le c\\le n\\}\\) require at least \\(n-1\\) distinct tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A tile cannot contain both an \\(L\\)-square and an \\(R\\)-square  \n\nSuppose a tile \\(T\\) contains an \\(L\\)-square \\(L_c=(r_{c+1},c)\\) and an \\(R\\)-square \\(R_{c'}=(r_{c'-1},c')\\).  \n\nIf \\(c<c'\\), then the column interval of \\(T\\) contains the column \\(c+1\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) contains the row \\(r_{c+1}\\), the uncovered square \\((r_{c+1},c+1)\\) would be covered – contradiction.  \n\nIf \\(c'>c\\) the same argument with the roles reversed yields a contradiction.  \nHence a tile can belong to at most one of the two families \\(\\{L_c\\}\\) and \\(\\{R_c\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Lower bound for the total number of tiles  \n\nFrom Sections 4, 5 and 6 we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **any** tiling satisfying the problem’s condition uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nU_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i\\le c-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers exactly the squares of column \\(c\\) **above** the diagonal.  \n\nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid c+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers exactly the squares of column \\(c\\) **below** the diagonal.\n\nAll rectangles are axis‑aligned, have disjoint interiors, avoid every diagonal square, and together cover every non‑diagonal square of the board.  \nThe number of rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{above the diagonal}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{below the diagonal}}=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nThe minimum possible number of tiles equals the lower bound \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\#\\text{tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
