 column c, define r_c = pi^{-1}(c). So r_c is the row where the uncovered square is in column c.\n\nDefine L_c = (r_{c+1}, c) for c = 1,...,n-1. Since pi(r_{c+1}) = c+1 > c, L_c is left of uncovered square in row r_{c+1}. Similarly, define R_c = (r_{c-1}, c) for c = 2,...,n. Since pi(r_{c-1}) = c-1 < c, R_c is right of uncovered square in row r_{c-1}.\n\nThus L_c are squares just left of the uncovered squares in each row (except the first column?), and R_c are squares just right of uncovered squares in each row (except the last column?). Actually L_c lies in column c, row r_{c+1}. So it's the square directly left of the uncovered square in row r_{c+1} (since uncovered square is at column c+1). Similarly, R_c is directly right of uncovered square in row r_{c-1}.\n\nNow the argument: Any tile covering L_c must have rightmost column exactly c, because if it extended to column c+1, it would cover the uncovered square (r_{c+1}, c+1). So tile covering L_c cannot extend beyond column c. Since L_c is in column c, the tile's rightmost column must be c. Similarly, any tile covering R_c must have leftmost column exactly c.\n\nThus each L_c must be covered by a distinct tile, because if a tile covered L_c and L_{c'} with c<c', then its rightmost column must be c' (by covering L_{c'}), but then it would also include column c+1, covering the uncovered square at (r_{c+1},c+1). So cannot. So each L_c requires a distinct tile. There are n-1 L_c squares, so at least n-1 distinct tiles. Similarly, each R_c requires a distinct tile, at least n-1 distinct tiles. Also a tile cannot cover both an L and an R square, because that would cause covering uncovered squares. So total tiles >= (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2.\n\nThus lower bound is valid. The construction: identity permutation, uncovered squares are diagonal. For each column c (2 <= c <= n), place vertical rectangle covering squares above diagonal in column c: squares (i,c) for i <= c-1. That's a rectangle of height c-1, width 1. For each column c (1 <= c <= n-1), place vertical rectangle covering squares below diagonal: squares (i,c) for i >= c+1. That's a rectangle of height n-c, width 1. So total 2n-2 rectangles. They cover all squares except diagonal. They are disjoint, because each column has two rectangles: one above diagonal, one below diagonal, but they don't overlap because they are separated by the diagonal square. Actually they are disjoint because they occupy distinct rows: above diagonal rectangle occupies rows 1..c-1, below diagonal occupies rows c+1..n. No overlap. So they are disjoint. So indeed we have a tiling with 2n-2 tiles.\n\nThus answer 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to check reasoning validity: The solution uses case analysis, definitions, lemmas, and a construction. It uses valid methods: combinatorial reasoning, proof by contradiction (in lemmas), explicit construction. All are valid.\n\nNow we need to check presentation: Are there any justification gaps? Possibly the solution may not explicitly prove that the vertical rectangles are indeed tiles (i.e., rectangular shape) and that they cover all non-diagonal squares. But that is fairly straightforward. The solution states \"vertical rectangle\" covering squares above diagonal, which is a rectangle of width 1 and height c-1. That's a valid rectangular tile. It also says \"All rectangles are axis‑aligned, have disjoint interiors, avoid every diagonal square, and together cover every non‑diagonal square of the board.\" This is a claim; is it fully justified? It seems plausible. The solution does not provide a rigorous proof that all non-diagonal squares are covered, but it's clear from the description. This is a minor justification gap, but acceptable.\n\nNow we need to check if any construction claims are missing details. The solution claims \"For each column c with 2≤c≤n place the vertical rectangle U_c = {(i,c) | 1 ≤ i ≤ c-1}\" and \"For each column c with 1≤c≤n-1 place the vertical rectangle D_c = {(i,c) | c+1 ≤ i ≤ n}\". Thes