changing the number of tiles needed.  Hence the lower bound \\(2n-2\\) holds for every possible placement of the uncovered squares.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**  \n   The construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower bound shows no tiling can use fewer.  For \\(n=2025\\) this yields \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the statement must leave exactly one unit square uncovered in each row and each column.  \nConsequently there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(Each pair \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) lies in row \\(i\\) and column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the diagonal case  \n\nIf we permute the rows by a permutation \\(\\pi\\) and the columns by a permutation \\(\\tau\\), the set of uncovered squares becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\{(\\pi(i),\\tau(\\sigma(i)))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nApplying the row permutation \\(\\pi=\\sigma^{-1}\\) and the column permutation \\(\\tau=\\operatorname{id}\\) transforms (1) into  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nthe main diagonal.  \n\nAny tiling of the original board can be transformed by the same row and column permutations into a tiling of the board with uncovered squares \\(U'\\), preserving the number of tiles.  Conversely, a tiling for \\(U'\\) can be permuted back to a tiling for the original configuration.  Hence the minimal number of tiles depends only on \\(n\\) and not on the particular permutation \\(\\sigma\\).  From now on we assume the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nDefine the following rectangles (tiles).\n\n*For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)*  \n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle whose left side lies on the vertical grid line \\(x=i\\) and whose right side on \\(x=n\\).\n\n*For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\)*  \n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\\(V_j\\) is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle whose bottom side lies on the horizontal grid line \\(y=j\\) and whose top side on \\(y=n\\).\n\n**Verification.**  \n\n- *Disjointness.*  \n  A cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs to exactly one horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) (by (3)) and to no vertical tile (since \\(j\\not< i\\)).  \n  A cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) belongs to exactly one vertical tile \\(V_j\\) (by (4)) and to no horizontal tile.  \n  A diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) satisfies neither \\(i< i\\) nor \\(i> i\\); thus it belongs to none of the tiles.  \n  Hence the sets \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{V_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) form a partition of the board minus the diagonal.\n\n- *Coverage of all non\u2011diagonal cells.*  \n  By the previous point every cell with \\(i\\neq j\\) is covered by exactly one tile.\n\n- *Geometric validity.*  \n  Each \\(H_i\\) and \\(V_j\\) consists of a contiguous block of unit squares; therefore its sides lie on grid lines, and different tiles do not overlap.\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n| \\{H_i\\}|+|\\{V_j\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nConsider the set of cells just above the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA single axis\u2011aligned rectangle cannot contain two distinct cells of \\(A\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((k,k+1)\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nBecause a rectangle\u2019s rows and columns are intervals, \\(R\\) contains all rows \\(i,i+1,\\dots ,k\\) and all columns \\(i+1,i+2,\\dots ,k+1\\).  \nConsequently the cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) (which lies on the diagonal) belongs to \\(R\\).  \nBut diagonal cells are uncovered, contradicting the requirement that a tile may not cover an uncovered square. \u220e\n\nSince \\(|A|=n-1\\) and each rectangle can cover at most one element of \\(A\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover the whole upper\u2011triangular region \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\).\n\nDefine analogously the set of cells just below the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same argument shows that any rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(B\\); therefore at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for the lower\u2011triangular region \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}\\).\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nNo rectangle can contain cells from both the upper\u2011triangular and the lower\u2011triangular regions.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains a cell \\((p,q)\\) with \\(p<q\\) (upper) and a cell \\((r,s)\\) with \\(r>s\\) (lower).  \nThen the row interval of \\(R\\) contains all rows from \\(\\min\\{p,r\\}\\) to \\(\\max\\{p,r\\}\\), and the column interval contains all columns from \\(\\min\\{s,q\\}\\) to \\(\\max\\{s,q\\}\\).  \nSince \\(p<q\\) and \\(s<r\\), the two intervals intersect; let \\(t\\) be any integer belonging to both.  \nThen the cell \\((t,t)\\) lies in \\(R\\), a diagonal (uncovered) cell \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e\n\nConsequently the rectangles covering the upper\u2011triangular part are disjoint from those covering the lower\u2011triangular part.  Hence any tiling must use at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Optimality  \n\nThe construction of Section\u202f2 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while Section\u202f3 proves that no tiling can use fewer.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
