ells immediately right of the holes,\n   \\[\n   M_R=\\{(i,\\sigma(i)+1):\\sigma(i)<n\\},\n   \\]\n   shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to tile \\(R\\).\n\n   Hence any tiling of the whole board requires at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n   tiles.\n\n4. **Achieving the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\). Then \\(L\\) is the strict lower triangle and \\(R\\) the strict upper triangle.  \n   - For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\), place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r=c+1,\\dots ,n\\). This yields \\(n-1\\) tiles covering all of \\(L\\).  \n   - For each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\), place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r=1,\\dots ,c-1\\). This yields another \\(n-1\\) tiles covering all of \\(R\\).  \n\n   The tiles are pairwise disjoint, they cover every unit square except the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), and each row and column contains exactly one uncovered cell. The total number of tiles used is \\(2n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA *tile* is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines and whose interior consists of unit squares. Tiles are required to be pairwise disjoint (no unit square is covered by more than one tile).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\n\nBecause each of the \\(n\\) rows must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, for each row \\(i\\) there is a unique column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) such that the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is uncovered.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered cell, the map \\(i\\mapsto\\sigma(i)\\) is a bijection of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); i.e. \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.\n\nThus the set of uncovered cells is  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Two disjoint regions\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery unit square not in \\(U\\) belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); the three sets \\(L,R,U\\) partition the board.\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{No tile can contain a cell of }L\\text{ and a cell of }R\\text{ simultaneously.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\) contains a cell \\((i_1,j_1)\\in L\\) and a cell \\((i_2,j_2)\\in R\\).  \nHence \\(j_1<\\sigma(i_1)\\) and \\(j_2>\\sigma(i_2)\\).  \nLet the row interval of \\(T\\) be \\([a,b]\\) and the column interval be \\([c,d]\\); then\n\\(a\\le i_1,i_2\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le j_1,j_2\\le d\\).  \nConsequently \\(c\\le j_1<\\sigma(i_1)\\) and \\(j_2>\\sigma(i_2)\\le d\\), so the column interval \\([c,d]\\) contains the integer \\(\\sigma(i_1)\\) (because \\(c\\le\\sigma(i_1)-1<\\sigma(i_1)\\le d\\)) and also the integer \\(\\sigma(i_2)\\).  \n\nConsider the row \\(r\\) with \\(a\\le r\\le b\\) such that \\(\\sigma(r)\\) lies between \\(c\\) and \\(d\\).  \nSince the set \\(\\{\\sigma(i)\\mid a\\le i\\le b\\}\\) contains both a value \\(<d\\) and a value \\(>c\\), by the pigeonhole principle there exists \\(r\\) with \\(\\sigma(r)\\in[c,d]\\).  \nThen the cell \\((r,\\sigma(r))\\) belongs to the rectangle \\(T\\), contradicting the fact that all holes are uncovered. ∎\n\nHence every tile is completely contained in either \\(L\\) or \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for each region\n\n##### 3.1  The left region  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) define the *critical* cell  \n\n\\[\nm_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1).\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM_L=\\{m_i\\mid \\sigma(i)>1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, exactly one index satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|M_L|=n-1. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nAny rectangle \\(T\\subseteq L\\) contains at most one cell of \\(M_L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\(T\\) contains two distinct cells \\(m_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and \\(m_{i'}=(i',\\sigma(i')-1)\\) with \\(i<i'\\).  \nSince both belong to \\(T\\), the column interval of \\(T\\) contains both \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')-1\\).  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(i')\\) then \\(\\sigma(i')-1\\ge\\sigma(i)\\); consequently the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i')-1)\\) lies in \\(T\\).  \nBut \\((i,\\sigma(i')-1)\\notin L\\) because its column is \\(\\ge\\sigma(i)\\). This contradicts \\(T\\subseteq L\\).  \nThe case \\(\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(i')\\) is symmetric. ∎\n\nSince every cell of \\(M_L\\) must be covered, Lemma 1 implies that at least \\(|M_L|=n-1\\) rectangles are required to tile \\(L\\).\n\n##### 3.2  The right region  \n\nDefine analogously  \n\n\\[\nM_R=\\{(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\mid \\sigma(i)<n\\},\n\\qquad |M_R|=n-1,\n\\]\n\nand apply the symmetric argument to obtain:\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nAny rectangle \\(T\\subseteq R\\) contains at most one cell of \\(M_R\\).\n\nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to tile \\(R\\).\n\n##### 3.3  Global lower bound  \n\nEvery tile lies in \\(L\\) or \\(R\\); thus the total number of tiles is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\}\\quad\\text{(strict lower triangle)},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}\\quad\\text{(strict upper triangle)}.\n\\]\n\n*Tiles covering \\(L\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{L}=\\{(r,c)\\mid r=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(L\\).\n\n*Tiles covering \\(R\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{R}=\\{(r,c)\\mid r=1,\\dots ,c-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint from each other and from the previous family, and they cover all cells of \\(R\\).\n\nThe uncovered cells are precisely the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), one in each row and column.  \nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound (5) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
