on).**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal is uncovered).  \n   - **Left tiles:** for each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a rectangle covering rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n     These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint and together cover all cells left of the diagonal.  \n   - **Right tiles:** for each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a rectangle covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,k\\) and columns \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n     These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint and together cover all cells right of the diagonal.\n\n   The two families are disjoint, they avoid the diagonal holes, and together they cover every other unit square. Thus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe consider an \\(n\\times n\\) board whose unit squares are indexed by \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square,\nthere exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation matrix; all other squares are covered by tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Decomposition into left and right parts  \n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i ,\\qquad 