"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Permutation of holes.**  \n   The condition \u201cexactly one uncovered square in each row and each column\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to be a permutation matrix: there exists a bijection \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(n=2025\\)) such that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n2. **Adjacent squares.**  \n   For every row \\(i\\) define the *left\u2011adjacent* square \\(\\alpha_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\) when \\(p(i)>1\\) and the *right\u2011adjacent* square \\(\\beta_i=(i,p(i)+1)\\) when \\(p(i)<n\\).  \n   Because \\(p\\) is a permutation, there are exactly \\(n-1\\) squares of each type, i.e. \\(2(n-1)\\) distinguished squares in total.\n\n3. **Lemma (no tile can contain two distinguished squares).**  \n   If a rectangular tile contains two distinct squares among the \\(\\alpha_i\\) and \\(\\beta_i\\), then it must also contain a hole \\((i,p(i))\\), contradicting the requirement that holes remain uncovered. The proof examines the three possible pairings (two \\(\\alpha\\)\u2019s, two \\(\\beta\\)\u2019s, one of each) and uses the fact that the column interval of a rectangle is contiguous.\n\n4. **Contradiction lower bound.**  \n   Suppose a tiling uses fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles. By the pigeonhole principle some tile would contain at least two of the \\(2(n-1)\\) distinguished squares, contradicting the lemma. Hence any admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal is uncovered).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a left tile covering rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a right tile covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,k\\) and columns \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   These \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, avoid all diagonal squares, and together cover every other unit square. Thus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum number of tiles required for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the unit squares of the \\(n\\times n\\) board by ordered pairs \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nu_i=(i,p(i)).\n\\]\n\nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na permutation matrix.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Distinguished adjacent squares  \n\nFor every row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,p(i)-1), & \\text{if }p(i)>1,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{undefined}, & \\text{if }p(i)=1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n\\beta_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,p(i)+1), & \\text{if }p(i)<n,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{undefined}, & \\text{if }p(i)=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, exactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=1\\) and exactly one satisfies \\(p(i)=n\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\{\\alpha_i\\}|=n-1,\\qquad |\\{\\beta_i\\}|=n-1,\n\\]\n\nso there are \\(2(n-1)\\) distinguished squares in total.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lemma: a tile cannot contain two distinguished squares  \n\n*Proof.*  Let \\(T\\) be a rectangular tile covering a set of rows \\(I=[r_1,r_2]\\) and a set of columns \\(J=[c_1,c_2]\\) (both intervals are inclusive).  \n\n- **Two left\u2011adjacent squares.**  \n  Suppose \\(T\\) contains \\(\\alpha_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\(\\alpha_j=(j,p(j)-1)\\) with \\(p(i)<p(j)\\).  \n  Then \\(c_1\\le p(i)-1<p(j)-1\\le c_2\\); hence the column \\(p(i)\\) lies in \\(J\\).  \n  Since row \\(i\\in I\\), the square \\((i,p(i))\\) is also covered by \\(T\\), contradicting the fact that holes must remain uncovered.\n\n- **Two right\u2011adjacent squares.**  \n  Suppose \\(T\\) contains \\(\\beta_i=(i,p(i)+1)\\) and \\(\\beta_j=(j,p(j)+1)\\) with \\(p(i)<p(j)\\).  \n  Then \\(c_1\\le p(i)+1<p(j)+1\\le c_2\\), so column \\(p(j)\\) belongs to \\(J\\).  \n  Because row \\(j\\in I\\), the hole \\((j,p(j))\\) would be covered, a contradiction.\n\n- **One left\u2011adjacent and one right\u2011adjacent square.**  \n  Suppose \\(T\\) contains \\(\\alpha_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\(\\beta_j=(j,p(j)+1)\\).  \n  Without loss of generality assume \\(p(i)\\le p(j)\\).  \n  Then \\(c_1\\le p(i)-1\\) and \\(c_2\\ge p(j)+1\\), so the interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) contains both \\(p(i)\\) and \\(p(j)\\).  \n  Consequently either \\((i,p(i))\\) or \\((j,p(j))\\) lies in \\(T\\), again a contradiction.\n\nThus a tile can contain **at most one** square from the set \\(\\{\\alpha_i\\}\\cup\\{\\beta_i\\}\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound via contradiction  \n\nAssume there exists a tiling of the board satisfying the problem conditions that uses fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nSince there are exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) distinguished squares and each tile can contain at most one of them (Lemma), the pigeonhole principle forces some tile to contain at least two distinguished squares.  \nBy the Lemma such a tile would also contain a hole, contradicting the requirement that holes remain uncovered.  \n\nHence any admissible tiling must contain at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge 2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\) (the main diagonal is uncovered).  \n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) define two rectangles:\n\n\\[\nL_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\},\n\\qquad\nR_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n- \\(L_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-k)\\times k\\) lying entirely to the left of the diagonal, because for any row \\(i\\ge k+1\\) we have \\(p(i)=i>k\\) and thus column \\(k\\) is strictly left of the hole \\((i,i)\\).  \n- \\(R_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\(k\\times (n-k)\\) lying entirely to the right of the diagonal, because for any row \\(i\\le k\\) we have \\(p(i)=i\\le k<k+1\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, they avoid all diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), and their union equals the set of all non\u2011diagonal squares:\n\n- If a square \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(j<i\\), set \\(k=j\\); then \\((i,j)\\in L_k\\).  \n- If a square \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(j>i\\), set \\(k=i\\); then \\((i,j)\\in R_k\\).\n\nThus the \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) diagonal squares, and each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \n\nTherefore a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction of \u00a75 exhibits a tiling that uses precisely \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
