                "content": ".\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares:**  \n   The condition “exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column’’ forces the uncovered squares to form a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound – column‑wise argument:**  \n   - For the *left* part of each row (columns \\(1,\\dots ,p(i)-1\\)) consider the cell \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) (it exists for every \\(p(i)>1\\)).  \n   - Any rectangle covering this cell cannot extend to column \\(p(i)\\) (the hole), hence its **rightmost column** is exactly \\(p(i)-1\\).  \n   - Since the values \\(p(i)-1\\) run through \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) exactly once, each integer \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) must be the rightmost column of a distinct rectangle.  \n   - Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all squares left of the holes.\n\n   - By symmetry, for the *right* part of each row (columns \\(p(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\)) the cell \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) forces the **leftmost column** of the rectangle covering it to be \\(p(i)+1\\).  \n   - The values \\(p(i)+1\\) also run through \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) exactly once, so at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for the right side.\n\n   - Hence any tiling uses at least \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound:**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - **Left of the diagonal:** For each column \\(j\\;(1\\le j\\le n-1)\\) place a vertical tile covering rows \\(j+1,\\dots ,n\\) in column \\(j\\). This is a \\( (n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle, and the \\(n-1\\) such tiles cover all squares strictly left of the diagonal.  \n   - **Right of the diagonal:** For each column \\(j\\;(2\\le j\\le n)\\) place a vertical tile covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,j-1\\) in column \\(j\\). This is a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle, and the \\(n-1\\) such tiles cover all squares strictly right of the diagonal.  \n\n   The two families are disjoint, together they use exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles and cover every square except the diagonal holes.  \n\n4. **Conclusion:** The lower bound \\(2(n-1)\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the statement leaves exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a **permutation matrix**: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nL_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad R_i:=n-p(i)\n\\]\n\nthe numbers of squares to the left and to the right of the hole in row \\(i\\).  \nThus the covered squares consist of the **left region**\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le L_i\\}\n\\]\n\nand the **right region**\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA tile is a rectangle, i.e. a set  \n\n\\[\nT=I\\times J=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in I,\\;j\\in J\\}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) intervals of consecutive integers.  \nIf a tile \\(T\\) contains a cell \\((i,j)\\in\\mathcal L\\) then, because the hole in row \\(i\\) lies at column \\(p(i)=L_i+1\\), the tile must satisfy \\(j\\le L_i\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\max J\\le \\min_{i\\in I}L_i. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, if a tile contains a cell of \\(\\mathcal R\\) then\n\n\\[\n\\min J\\ge \\max_{i\\in I}p(i)+1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is a unique row \\(i_j\\) with \\(L_{i_j}=j\\) (because the values \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) are precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)).  \nConsider the cell \\((i_j,j)\\in\\mathcal L\\).  \nAny tile covering this cell must satisfy (1) with \\(j\\) as the rightmost column of the tile; otherwise the tile would contain the hole \\((i_j,p(i_j))=(i_j,j+1)\\). Hence the **rightmost column** of the tile that covers \\((i_j,j)\\) is exactly \\(j\\).\n\nSince a tile has a unique rightmost column, distinct values \\(j\\) require distinct tiles.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are necessary to cover the whole left region \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the right region: for each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is a unique row \\(i'_j\\) with \\(p(i'_j)=j-1\\), i.e. with leftmost covered column \\(j\\). Any tile covering \\((i'_j,j)\\in\\mathcal R\\) must have **leftmost column** equal to \\(j\\). Hence at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nConsequently any tiling of the board that respects the permutation holes uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1) \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left of the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{L}}_j\\) is a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle; it contains no diagonal cell because its rows are all \\(>j\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T^{\\text{L}}_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) covers every square with column index smaller than its row index, i.e. the whole set \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Right of the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{R}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{R}}_j\\) is a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle; it avoids the diagonal because its rows are all \\(<j\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T^{\\text{R}}_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) covers every square with column index larger than its row index, i.e. the whole set \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint, together they consist of exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\n\\]\n\ntiles and cover all squares except the diagonal holes.  \nThus a tiling with \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nFrom (3) any tiling needs at least \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, and the construction above uses exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.  \nHence the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
