-----------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is a unique row \\(i_j\\) with \\(L_{i_j}=j\\) (because the values \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) are precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)).  \nConsider the cell \\((i_j,j)\\in\\mathcal L\\).  \nAny tile covering this cell must satisfy (1) with \\(j\\) as the rightmost column of the tile; otherwise the tile would contain the hole \\((i_j,p(i_j))=(i_j,j+1)\\). Hence the **rightmost column** of the tile that covers \\((i_j,j)\\) is exactly \\(j\\).\n\nSince a tile has a unique rightmost column, distinct values \\(j\\) require distinct tiles.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are necessary to cover the whole left region \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the right region: for each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is a unique row \\(i'_j\\) with \\(p(i'_j)=j-1\\), i.e. with leftmost covered column \\(j\\). Any tile covering \\((i'_j,j)\\in\\mathcal R\\) must have **leftmost column** equal to \\(j\\). Hence at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nConsequently any tiling of the board that respects the permutation holes uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1) \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left of the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{L}}_j\\) is a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle; it contains no diagonal cell because its rows are all \\(>j\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T^{\\text{L}}_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) covers every square with column index smaller than its row index, i.e. the whole set \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Right of the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2