Any tile covering this cell has rightmost column \\(j\\) by (1).  \nIf such a tile also contained row \\(r\\), then it would contain the hole \\((r,j)\\), which is forbidden.  \nTherefore every tile that covers the left cell lies **entirely below** row \\(r\\).\n\n*The right cell* in column \\(j\\) is \\((i',j)\\) where \\(p(i')=j-1\\).  \nAny tile covering this cell has leftmost column \\(j\\) by (2).  \nIf it contained row \\(r\\) it would again cover the hole \\((r,j)\\); hence every tile that covers the right cell lies **entirely above** row \\(r\\).\n\nBecause a rectangle\u2019s rows form a single interval, a tile cannot be simultaneously entirely above and entirely below row \\(r\\).  Hence the tile covering the left cell of column \\(j\\) is different from the tile covering the right cell of the same column.  \n\nThus the \\(n-1\\) tiles forced by the left cells and the \\(n-1\\) tiles forced by the right cells are all distinct.  Consequently any tiling must use at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Tiles left of the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_{j}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{L}}_{j}\\) is a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle; it contains no diagonal cell because its rows are all \\(>j\\).\n\n*Tiles right of the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{R}}_{j}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{R}}_{j}\\) is a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle; its rows are all \\(<j\\), so it avoids the diagonal cell \\((j,j)\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint: for a fixed column \\(j\\) the tile \\(T^{\\text{L}}_{j}\\) occupies rows below the diagonal, while \\(T^{\\text{R}}_{j}\\) occupies rows above the diagonal.  Together they cover every unit square except the diagonal ones, and no two tiles overlap.\n\nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}=2n-2=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (3) shows that any admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nThe construction above uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
