umns \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) (size \\(1\\times (n-k)\\)).  \n\n   The vertical family tiles all squares left of the diagonal, the horizontal family tiles all squares right of the diagonal; the two families are disjoint and together cover exactly the \\(n^{2}-n\\) squares that are not uncovered.  The total number of rectangles is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048 .\n   \\]\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Matilda must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(f\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,f(i))\\).  \nAll other squares must be covered by pairwise disjoint axis‑parallel rectangles (tiles).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i = f(i)-1\\qquad(\\text{if }f(i)>1),\\qquad\nr_i = f(i)+1\\qquad(\\text{if }f(i)<n).\n\\]\n\nThe cells immediately to the left and to the right of the uncovered square are  \n\n\\[\nL'=\\{(i,\\ell_i)\\mid f(i)>1\\},\\qquad   \nR'=\\{(i,r_i)\\mid f(i)<n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(f\\) is a bijection, exactly one row has \\(f(i)=1\\) (so it contributes no element to \\(L'\\)) and exactly one row has \\(f(i)=n\\) (so it contributes no element to \\(R'\\)).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n|L'|=|R'|=n-1,\\qquad |L'\\cup R'|=2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma.**  \nLet \\(Q\\) be a rectangle whose interior contains no uncovered square \\((i,f(i))\\).  \nThen \\(Q\\) contains **at most one** element of \\(L'\\cup R'\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(Q\\) contains two distinct cells of \\(L'\\); write them as \\((i,f(i)-1)\\) and \\((j