angle is a single column \\(k\\); its rows are all those strictly **below** the hole \\((k,k)\\).  Since the hole in column \\(k\\) lies in row \\(k\\), which is not part of \\(V_k\\), the rectangle contains no uncovered square.\n\n*Right of the diagonal.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_k=\\{(k,j)\\mid j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\(1\\times (n-k)\\).  Its row is the row \\(k\\) and its columns are all those strictly **to the right** of the hole \\((k,k)\\); again no uncovered square lies inside.\n\nThe family \\(\\{V_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) tiles every square left of the diagonal, and the family \\(\\{H_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) tiles every square right of the diagonal.  The two families are disjoint, and together they cover all \\(n^{2}-n\\) squares that are not uncovered.  \n\nThe total number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nInequality (2) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, while construction (3) shows that \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\#\\text{tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
