olumn must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the matrix \\(J-I\\)\n\nPermute the columns according to \\(\\pi^{-1}\\); i.e. replace column \\(j\\) by column \\(\\pi^{-1}(j)\\).  \nColumn permutation does not change the number of tiles needed, because it merely re‑orders whole columns.  \nAfter this permutation the uncovered squares become the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\); consequently the squares that must be tiled are exactly the off‑diagonal cells.\n\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) matrix  \n\n\\[\nM=(m_{ij}),\\qquad \nm_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n1 & (i\\neq j)\\\\[2pt]\n0 & (i=j).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nA tile is an axis‑aligned rectangle, i.e. a set of cells of the form  \n\n\\[\nR\\times C=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are **intervals** of consecutive indices (the rows and columns of a tile are consecutive).  \nSince the diagonal cells are forbidden, a rectangle may be used only when  \n\n\\[\nR\\cap C=\\varnothing .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if some index \\(k\\) belongs to both \\(R\\) and \\(C\\), then the cell \\((k,k)\\) would be inside the rectangle, contradicting (1).  \n\nThus the problem is: partition the set of 1‑entries of \\(M\\) into rectangles satisfying (1).  \nAny such partition yields a tiling of the board, and conversely every tiling gives such a partition.  \nHence the minimum number of tiles equals the minimum number of rectangles needed to partition the off‑diagonal entries of \\(M\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that any partition of the off‑diagonal entries of \\(M\\) into rectangles satisfying (1) uses at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n*Base case \\(n=2\\).*  \n\\(M=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\[2pt]1&0\\end{pmatrix}\\).  \nA rectangle obeying (1) can contain at most one of the two 1‑entries, because a rectangle containing both would need rows \\(\\{1,2\\}\\) and columns \\(\\{1,2\\}\\), which intersect.  \nHence at least two rectangles are necessary, and \\(2\\cdot2-2=2\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for size \\(n\\) (\\(n\\ge 2\\)).  \nConsider a partition of the \\((n+1)\\times (n+1)\\) matrix \\(M_{n+1}=J_{n+1}-I_{n+1}\\).\n\nThe last row (row \\(n+1\\)) contains the 1‑entries \\((n+1,j)\\) for all \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nBecause of (1), any rectangle that contains one of these cells cannot contain column \\(n+1\\); consequently every such rectangle has the form  \n\n\\[\n\\{n+1\\}\\times C,\\qquad C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll the 1‑entries of the last row are disjoint, so they can be covered by a **single** rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\text{row}}=\\{n+1\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus any partition must contain at least one rectangle of this type.\n\nAnalogously, the last column (column \\(n+1\\)) contains the 1‑entries \\((i,n+1)\\) for \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nEach rectangle covering one of them must be of the form  \n\n\\[\nA\\times\\{n+1\\},\\qquad A\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nand all these cells can be covered by the single rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\text{col}}=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence any partition contains at least the two rectangles \\(R_{\\text{row}}\\) and \\(R_{\\text{col}}\\).\n\nDelete row \\(n+1\\) and column \\(n+1\\) from the board and also delete the two rectangles just described.  \nWhat remains is a partition of the off‑diagonal entries of the \\(n\\times n\\) matrix \\(M_n\\).  \nBy the induction hypothesis this sub‑partition uses at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\nTherefore the original partition uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(2n-2)+2 = 2(n+1)-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.  This completes the induction, proving that **any** partition requires at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Upper bound: a partition with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\nWe now exhibit a concrete partition attaining the lower bound.\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_i = \\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(R_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle lying entirely above the diagonal; it contains precisely the 1‑entries \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\).\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_j = \\{j,j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j-1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(C_j\\) is an \\((n-j+1)\\times 1\\) rectangle lying entirely below the diagonal; it contains precisely the 1‑entries \\((i,j-1)\\) with \\(i>j-1\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{C_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint:\n\n* Two distinct rectangles \\(R_i,R_{i'}\\) use different rows, so they cannot intersect.\n* Two distinct rectangles \\(C_j,C_{j'}\\) use different columns, so they cannot intersect.\n* A rectangle \\(R_i\\) uses row \\(i\\) and columns larger than \\(i\\); a rectangle \\(C_j\\) uses column \\(j-1\\) and rows larger than \\(j-1\\).  \n  If a cell belonged to both, we would have simultaneously \\(j-1>i\\) (because the column of \\(C_j\\) is larger than the row of \\(R_i\\)) and \\(i>j-1\\) (because the row of \\(C_j\\) is larger than the column of \\(R_i\\)), an impossibility.  \n\nConsequently the \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles are disjoint and together cover every off‑diagonal entry of \\(M\\):  \n\n* If \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in R_i\\).  \n* If \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in C_j\\).\n\nThus we have a tiling of the board with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangular tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nBoth the lower b