ns exactly one uncovered square. Thus a tiling with \\(45+45=90\\) tiles exists.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound (90) is attainable, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(90\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   The uncovered squares form a permutation\n\nIn each row exactly one unit square is left uncovered, and each column also contains exactly one uncovered square.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nWe call \\(\\sigma\\) the **hole\u2011permutation**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Reduction to anchored rectangles\n\nA rectangle used in a tiling is a product \\(R\\times C\\) where  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{r_{1},r_{1}+1,\\dots ,r_{2}\\},\\qquad \nC=\\{c_{1},c_{1}+1,\\dots ,c_{2}\\}\n\\]\n\nare intervals of rows and columns.  \nIf a rectangle lies completely to the left of a hole in each of its rows, then its column interval does **not** contain the column of that hole.  \nHence the rectangle can be extended leftwards until column\u202f1 without meeting any hole.  \nThe same argument works for rectangles lying to the right of all holes in their rows, which can be extended rightwards until column\u202f\\(n\\).\n\nThus we may assume that every rectangle of a feasible tiling is of one of the two types  \n\n* **left\u2011anchored:** \\(C=[1,c]\\) for some \\(c<n\\);\n* **right\u2011anchored:** \\(C=[d,n]\\) for some \\(d>1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Record minima and maxima\n\nFor the permutation \\(\\sigma\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL=\\#\\{\\text{left\u2011to\u2011right minima of }\\sigma\\},\\qquad\nM=\\#\\{\\text{left\u2011to\u2011right maxima of }\\sigma\\}.\n\\]\n\nScanning the rows from top to bottom, let  \n\n\\[\nw_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n)\n\\]\n\nbe the width of the left part of row \\(i\\).  \nIf row \\(i\\) is a left\u2011to\u2011right minimum, then \\(w_i\\) is **strictly smaller** than every preceding \\(w_j\\) (\\(j<i\\)).  \nConsequently a left\u2011anchored rectangle can never cover rows both before and after such a row, because its column interval would have to be at most \\(w_i\\), which is too short for the earlier rows.  \nHence a new left\u2011anchored rectangle must start at each left\u2011to\u2011right minimum.  \nAnalogously, a new right\u2011anchored rectangle must start at each left\u2011to\u2011right maximum (the right part of a row has width \\(n-\\sigma(i)\\)).\n\nTherefore any tiling uses at least \\(L\\) left\u2011anchored and at least \\(M\\) right\u2011anchored rectangles, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{T\\ge L+M}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   A lower bound for \\(L+M\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_{1}>a_{2}>\\dots >a_{L}\n\\]\n\nbe the values of the minima, and set \\(a_{L+1}=0\\).  \nThe intervals  \n\n\\[\nI_{k}=(a_{k+1},a_{k}]\\qquad (1\\le k\\le L)\n\\]\n\nform a partition of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nb_{1}<b_{2}<\\dots <b_{M}\n\\]\n\nbe the values of the maxima, and set \\(b_{0}=0\\).  \nThe intervals  \n\n\\[\nJ_{\\ell}=(b_{\\ell-1},b_{\\ell}]\\qquad (1\\le \\ell\\le M)\n\\]\n\nalso partition \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nEvery integer \\(x\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) belongs to exactly one interval \\(I_{k}\\) and exactly one interval \\(J_{\\ell}\\); thus it determines a pair \\((k,\\ell)\\).  \nSince there are only \\(L\\cdot M\\) possible pairs, we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n\\le L\\cdot M}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) and the arithmetic\u2013geometric mean inequality,\n\n\\[\nL+M\\ge \\big\\lfloor\\sqrt n\\big\\rfloor+\\big\\lceil\\sqrt n\\big\\rceil\n      =\\big\\lceil2\\sqrt n\\big\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   Numerical lower bound for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nBecause \\(2025=45^{2}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\big\\lceil2\\sqrt{2025}\\big\\rceil\n   =\\big\\lceil2\\cdot45\\big\\rceil=90 .\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (3) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{T\\ge 90}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.   Construction attaining \\(90\\) tiles\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\na=b=45,\\qquad n=ab=2025 .\n\\]\n\nPartition \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) into the \\(b\\) blocks  \n\n\\[\nB_{k}=\\{(k-1)a+1,\\dots ,ka\\}\\qquad (1\\le k\\le b).\n\\]\n\nInside each block write the numbers in **decreasing** order and concatenate the blocks.  \nThe resulting permutation \\(\\sigma\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sigma=&\\;(45,44,\\dots ,1,\\;\n          90,89,\\dots ,46,\\;\n          \\dots ,\\\\\n        &\\;2025,2024,\\dots ,1981).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*The left\u2011to\u2011right minima.*  \nThe first block \\(B_{1}\\) is strictly decreasing, so each of its \\(a=45\\) entries is a new minimum. No later entry can be a minimum because every later block contains only larger numbers. Hence  \n\n\\[\nL=45 .\n\\]\n\n*The left\u2011to\u2011right maxima.*  \nThe first element of each block is larger than all numbers that precede it, so each block contributes one new maximum. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nM=45 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L+M=90\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 6.1   The explicit tiling\n\nLet the rows be numbered \\(1,\\dots ,n\\).  \nDenote by \\(i_{1}<i_{2}<\\dots <i_{L}\\) the rows of the minima (these are precisely the rows of block \\(B_{1}\\)).  \nFor \\(r=1,\\dots ,L\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{left}}_{r}= \\{\\,i_{r},i_{r}+1,\\dots ,i_{r+1}-1\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nC^{\\text{left}}_{r}= \\{\\,1,2,\\dots ,\\sigma(i_{r})-1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(i_{L+1}=n+1\\).  \nEach set \\(R^{\\text{left}}_{r}\\times C^{\\text{left}}_{r}\\) is a left\u2011anchored rectangle; the collection \\(\\{R^{\\text{left}}_{r}\\times C^{\\text{left}}_{r}\\}_{r=1}^{L}\\) covers **all** squares lying to the left of the holes.\n\nSimilarly, let \\(j_{1}<j_{2}<\\dots <j_{M}\\) be the rows of the maxima (the first rows of the blocks).  \nFor \\(s=1,\\dots ,M\\) set  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{right}}_{s}= \\{\\,j_{s},j_{s}+1,\\dots ,j_{s+1}-1\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nC^{\\text{right}}_{s}= \\{\\,\\sigma(j_{s})+1,\\dots ,n\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(j_{M+1}=n+1\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(R^{\\text{right}}_{s}\\times C^{\\text{right}}_{s}\\) are right\u2011anchored and together cover all squares lying to the right of the holes.\n\nThe two families are disjoint (their column intervals are disjoint), and together they cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nThus we obtain a tiling with exactly  \n\n\\[\nL+M=45+45=90\n\\]\n\nrectangular tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.   Optimality\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Every feasible tiling uses at least \\(90\\) tiles (Section\u202f5).\n* There exists a tiling with exactly \\(90\\) tiles (Section\u202f6).\n\nHence \\(90\\) is the minimal possible number.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{90}\n\\]"
